      "level": 1,
      "content": "Using Alert"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "The alert() method of the window object allows you to display text in a simple alert box."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Example"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "html",
      "content": "1<button id=\"btn\">Greet</button>"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "content": "1const btn = document.querySelector(\"#btn\");\n2\n3btn.addEventListener(\"click\", () => {\n4    window.alert(\"Hello!\");\n5});"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Run the above code and click the button."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "You'll see the text \"Hello!\" appear in an alert box."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Notice alert() and console.log() are quite similar, as both simply display the text specified inside them."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Next, let's see a practical example of where you might use alert()."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "content": "How does it work?"
    },
